// Interviewer: "Write a method to find duplicate elements in a list"
// Bad approach - fresher immediately writes:
public List<Integer> findDuplicates(List<Integer> numbers) {
    var duplicates = new ArrayList<Integer>();
    var seen = new HashSet<Integer>();
    for (var num : numbers) {
        if (seen.contains(num)) {
            duplicates.add(num); // Bug: adds duplicate multiple times
        }
        seen.add(num);
    }
    return duplicates;
}

// Problem: Didn't clarify if duplicates should appear once or match frequency
// Problem: Didn't ask about null handling, empty list, or order preservation
// Problem: Jumped to implementation without discussing approach first

// Better approach - think aloud first:
// "Should each duplicate appear once in result, or multiple times?"
// "Do we need to preserve original order or can result be unordered?"
// "Can the input list contain nulls?"
// Then: "I'm thinking a HashSet to track seen elements, and..."

// BAD project description on resume:
// "E-commerce Application"
// - Built using Java, Spring Boot, MySQL
// - Implemented REST APIs
// - Used Postman for testing
// Problem: No context, scale, or specific technical decisions shown

// GOOD project description on resume:
// "E-commerce Order Management System | Java 17, Spring Boot, PostgreSQL"
// - Designed REST API handling product catalog, cart, and order workflows
//   serving 100+ test users with average response time under 200ms
// - Implemented @Transactional order processing to ensure atomicity when
//   updating inventory and creating payment records
// - Used Spring Data JPA with optimized queries, reducing N+1 query issues
//   in product-category joins (reduced from 50+ queries to 2 per page load)
// - GitHub: github.com/yourname/ecommerce-api (150+ commits, README with setup)

// Key differences:
// 1. Quantified scale (100 users, 200ms, 150 commits)
// 2. Named specific technical choices (@Transactional, JPA optimizations)
// 3. Showed problem-solving (N+1 query reduction)
// 4. Included verifiable proof (GitHub link with activity)

// Question: "Tell me about a time you debugged a difficult issue"

// BAD ANSWER (generic, hypothetical, no specifics):
// "When I face bugs, I first try to reproduce the issue, then I check
// the logs and use debugging tools. I google the error message and look
// at Stack Overflow. If that doesn't work, I ask my teammates for help.
// Usually we figure it out by working together."
// Problem: Hypothetical "I would" instead of real "I did"
// Problem: No specific situation, technology, or outcome
// Problem: "We figured it out" - unclear what YOU did

// GOOD ANSWER (specific situation with STAR structure):
// "In my library management project built with Spring Boot, users reported
// books weren't getting marked as returned even after submitting the form.
// (Situation) I was responsible for the book transaction service.
// (Task) I reproduced the issue and found no errors in logs, which was
// confusing because the endpoint was being called. Using Spring Boot
// Actuator and adding debug logs, I discovered @Transactional wasn't
// propagating properly because I'd accidentally made the method private
// instead of public. (Action) Once I fixed the access modifier and
// understood transaction proxy requirements, I also added integration
// tests to catch this class of error. (Result) The bug was fixed and
// I learned that Spring AOP requires public methods for proxying,
// which I now check in all my transactional code."

// Freshers can define polymorphism but stumble when asked to explain this:

interface PaymentProcessor {
    void processPayment(double amount);
}

class CreditCardProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    public void processPayment(double amount) {
        // Validate card, charge via payment gateway
        System.out.println("Charging $" + amount + " to credit card");
    }
}

class PayPalProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    public void processPayment(double amount) {
        // OAuth flow, PayPal API call
        System.out.println("Charging $" + amount + " via PayPal");
    }
}

// Polymorphism in action - OrderService doesn't know implementation details:
class OrderService {
    private final PaymentProcessor processor; // Interface reference

    public OrderService(PaymentProcessor processor) {
        this.processor = processor; // Could be any implementation
    }

    public void checkout(double total) {
        processor.processPayment(total); // Runtime binding decides which method
    }
}

// At runtime: new OrderService(new CreditCardProcessor()) or new PayPalProcessor()
// Key: OrderService code never changes when adding new payment methods
// Gap exposed: "Why use interface here instead of concrete CreditCardProcessor?"
// Answer: To allow swapping implementations without changing OrderService code
